/*
 * The derivative, as a library (design 4.4, `docs/design/program-transforms/README.cn.md`).
 *
 *     vjp(f) = transpose(split(reinterp<jvp>(f)))
 *     grad(f)              -- `vjp` read at one number, the seed 1
 *
 * This is the assembly the design asks for, written out rather than described. `split(jvp(f))`
 * answers the forward half, and the forward half called at the point answers `(value, lin)`: the
 * value, and the linear one. `lin` is the backward graph with the residuals already in its closure,
 * so its parameters are the tangents and it reads nothing else. `transpose(lin)` is therefore the
 * shape that takes the cotangent and answers the cotangents -- `(ct) => (cotangents)`, one per
 * tangent -- which is the shape `transpose` has for every function since the author's ruling of
 * 2026-11-02 11:30: every parameter of the transposed function is a linear input, and a value its
 * body needs is captured by the function that is transposed, not passed to it.
 *
 * The `with` list is not one of those parameters: it is a residual like everything else the second
 * half reads (author's ruling of 2026-11-03 15:15: 带 `with` 的函数，pullback 带不带 `with` 列表——
 * 不带，闭包捕获). `split` captures the `with` value the backward half reads in `lin`'s closure, so
 * `lin` is a function of the tangents alone and `transpose(lin)` is the declared `Pullback<F>`
 * `(ct) => (...Tangent<ParamsOf<F>>)` -- `stdlib/types.cml`. The `with` values are written once, in
 * the call that supplies them to `vjp`: `let (y, pb) = vjp(scale)<2.0>(3.0); pb(1.0)`.
 *
 * The surface is JAX's (author's ruling of 2026-11-01): `vjp(f)(x)` is the pair `(y, pullback)` and
 * `pullback(ct)` is the cotangent of `f`'s parameters, so `grad(f)(x)` is `vjp(f)(x).1(1.0)`.
 *
 * The module imports the rulesets it composes. `jvp` walks forward with the rules in
 * `rulesets.jvp` and `transpose` walks the backward half with the rules in `rulesets.transpose`,
 * so those two imports are part of this file and not of the program that calls it: a library that
 * makes its caller know how it is put together is not a library, the same reading as a pass's
 * precondition. Take either import out and the pullback raises at the call below instead, on this
 * file's own line: `transpose has no transpose for this function` without `rulesets.transpose`, and
 * `jvp has no forward rule for ':op/mul_d'` without `rulesets.jvp` -- which is what
 * `test/cases/compile/transforms/vjp_carries_its_ruleset.cml` holds it to.
 *
 * The signatures are stand-ins for `Vjp<F>`, `Split<F>` and `ParamsOf<F>` (design 6.1 is their
 * step), so `vjp` is declared once per arity rather than once for every `F` -- the same rule
 * `tensor` follows for a name that is declared once per type it accepts. The generic form replaces
 * these declarations, not their bodies, and the names stay in any case: a program says `grad`, and
 * which transforms answer it is the library's business (author, 2026-10-31).
 */

import rulesets.jvp
import rulesets.transpose
import { is_function, ParamsOf, Vjp, WithOf } from types
import { jvp, split, transpose } from transforms

/// The reverse transform, in JAX's order (author's ruling of 2026-11-01): `vjp(f)(x)` gives the pair
/// `(y, pullback)`, and `pullback(ct)` gives the cotangents of `f`'s parameters, `ct`'s own weight one
/// member per parameter. The pullback is `lin`'s transpose, and that is the whole of the step: the
/// two have the shape `(ct) => (cotangents)` (author's ruling of 2026-11-02 11:30), so nothing is
/// wrapped around it.
///
/// The pullback answers a tuple in every case, one member per parameter, so a function of one
/// parameter has a one-long one -- `(27.0)` for `vjp(cube)(3.0)`'s pullback at `ct = 1.0` -- and the
/// number a gradient is comes out of it by position: `grad(f)(x)` is `vjp(f)(x).1(1.0).0` (author's
/// ruling of 2026-11-01 14:30: one rule for every arity, no exception for one parameter, no type
/// function that takes an element out of a one-long tuple).
/// The derivative of `f` with respect to its parameters: `vjp(f)(x, y, ...)` is the pair
/// `(value, pullback)`, and `pullback(ct)` answers the cotangents of the parameters -- one member per
/// parameter, so a function of one parameter has a one-long tuple (author's ruling of 2026-11-01: one
/// rule for every arity, no exception for one parameter, no type function that takes an element out of
/// a one-long tuple).
///
/// One declaration for every arity: `F` is the function, `Vjp<F>` says the parameters are `F`'s and the
/// pullback's cotangents are too (stdlib/types.cml), and `<...ws> (...xs)` takes both lists the
/// instantiated `Vjp<F>` has -- the `with` list and the parameters, each from the position the lambda
/// stands in (author's rulings of 2026-11-01 19:10 and 2026-11-03 13:50). The packs are values in the
/// body: `xs` is the tuple of the parameters and `ws` the tuple of `f`'s `with` values, so
/// `split(jvp(f))<...ws>(...xs)` hands the forward half the list it declares and the parameters it
/// takes. `transpose(lin)` takes no `with` argument and none is written for it: `lin` has no `with`
/// list, because the `with` value the backward half reads is a residual the cut captured in its
/// closure (author's ruling of 2026-11-03 15:15; `Pullback<F>` in stdlib/types.cml is
/// `(ct) => (...Tangent<ParamsOf<F>>)`, no list). So the `with` values are written once, where the
/// lambda receives them, and `split(jvp(f))<...ws>(...xs)` is the only place they appear. The lambda
/// writes the pack rather than naming the `with` parameters: `Vjp<F>` splices `WithOf<F>` as types,
/// and a body that read a name from there would depend on what the caller of `vjp` called its own
/// parameters.
/// The whole reading in one line: the function is written where the call is, so the example is a
/// program of its own -- `y` is `x * x` at 3, and the pullback read at `ct = 1.0` answers the
/// derivative there, one member per parameter.
/// @example let (y, pullback) = vjp((x: float) => x * x)(3.0)    // y = 9.0, and pullback(1.0) is (6.0)
export func vjp::<F>(f: F): Vjp<F> when is_function::<F>() {
    return <...ws> (...xs) => {
        let (y, lin) = split(jvp(f))<...ws>(...xs)
        (y, transpose(lin))
    }
}
/// The gradient of a function of one parameter: `vjp` with the seed 1, its second half taken, and the
/// one member a one-parameter pullback answers read out by position --
/// `grad(f)(x)` is `vjp(f)(x).1(1.0).0` (author's ruling of 2026-11-01 14:30, the 06:50 formula with
/// the `.0` added: the pullback always answers a tuple). Read `grad(f)` as a name for that reading of
/// `vjp`, not as a second transform.
/// @example-fragment `f` is a function value, so the example is a fragment of a program that declares one
/// @example let slope = grad(f)
export func grad(f: (x: float) => float): (x: float) => float {
    let reading = vjp(f)
    return (x: float) => {
        let (value, pullback) = reading(x)
        let _ = value
        let cotangents = pullback(1.0)
        cotangents.0
    }
}

/// The value and the gradient of `f`'s first `with` parameter -- the shape the engine's `with_grad`
/// answers (`stdlib/signatures/autodiff.cml`), written as the composition design principles 9.9 asks
/// for. **The name refuses because its body has not been written**: what stands here is a
/// placeholder in this file, and this paragraph says what writing it takes, so that the refusal is
/// read as a to-do of the library and not as a limit of the composition.
///
/// The composition is one step: `vjp` differentiates the ordinary parameters only, so the weight is
/// moved to one by a lambda -- `vjp((ww: W) => f<ww>(...))(w)` -- and the gradient is the seed 1
/// read off the pullback. That step answers the number today. Since `lane-withtan` (merged as
/// `713e1ff26`) a `with` argument keeps its direction through a lifted call, so `weighted`
/// (`x * y + x`) at a weight of 3.0 answers 3.0 through the composition -- the engine's own number,
/// pinned by `test/cases/compile/transforms/library_grad_of_a_with_weight.cml`. The zero this name
/// used to be justified by was the state before that merge, and it is not what the composition does
/// now.
///
/// What this file is missing is its own two things: the body that answers the `(value, gradient)`
/// pair, and the declaration whose second member is the weight's gradient type
/// (`FirstOf<WithOf<F>>`, design principles 5.6, `stdlib/types.cml`). The general form of the body
/// calls the function value `f` from a body the lift has to walk -- the gap `lane-lgrad` records --
/// and the parameter list that would write `f`'s `with` pack is the paused piece
/// `docs/handover/reports/2026-11-02-a-lambdas-rest-parameter.cn.md` measures. Until both are in
/// place the name answers `never`, the one type that promises no number.
///
/// The other shapes a `with_grad` is asked for stop before a pair could be written, and each one
/// names where it stops instead of answering a number: a `with` parameter that is a struct, and a
/// function of two `with` parameters with no ordinary parameter, both stop in `jvp`; a tensor
/// `with` parameter whose lambda captures an array stops in `split`.
///
/// The result type says only what is true of this call: it never gives a value. The engine's
/// transform has a declaration of its own -- `ValueAndGradient<F>`, whose second member is the type
/// of the *first* member of `F`'s `with` list -- and the type can be named: `stdlib/types.cml`
/// writes `ParamsOf<F>`, `WithOf<F>`, `ResultOf<F>`, `Tangent<T>` and `FirstOf<P>`, the head of a
/// pack taken apart by a `match type` case (design principles 5.6, `FirstOf<WithOf<F>>`). What is
/// written here is still `never`: a declaration that promised a number would be an approximation of
/// the shape it claims until the body beside it answers one, and `never` is neither.
///
/// The four groups to measure the body on when it is written are the four groups of
/// `test/cases/modules/autodiff/grad_first_with_parameter.cml`, and the entry that holds this
/// refusal is `test/cases/compile/transforms/with_grad_from_the_library.cml`.
/// The example is a program of its own and stops at the name: it writes a call that is a value, and it
/// reads nothing out of it, because the function `with_grad` answers returns `never` -- a program that
/// asked `frozen(3.0)` for a number would not compile, and one that ran would stop with the sentence
/// above. The gradient of a first `with` parameter is read with `grad`: `grad(weighted)<3.0>(2.0)`.
/// @example let frozen = with_grad((y: float) => y * 2.0)
export func with_grad::<F>(f: F): <...WithOf<F>> (...ParamsOf<F>) => never when is_function::<F>() {
    let _ = f
    return <...ws> (...xs) => {
        fail<'RuntimeError'>(
            'This name refuses because the body behind it has not been written: it is a placeholder in this library file, not a limit of the composition. `with_grad` is `vjp` of a lambda that moves the weight onto an ordinary parameter, and that composition carries a weight direction today -- `weighted` at a weight of 3.0 answers the gradient 3.0 through it, which is the engine answer. What is missing is the pair body and the declaration it answers with (`FirstOf<WithOf<F>>`, `stdlib/types.cml`). Until they are written, read the gradient of a first `with` parameter with `grad(f)<w>(...)` from the autodiff module, and the derivatives of the ordinary parameters with `vjp(f)`.')
    }
}
